1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,215
A speedometer shows sixty.

2
00:00:02,215 --> 00:00:05,154
The wind report says
twenty, from the west.

3
00:00:05,554 --> 00:00:07,385
One number has no direction.

4
00:00:07,385 --> 00:00:08,738
The other one does.

5
00:00:08,738 --> 00:00:10,851
Does that small difference matter?

6
00:00:11,301 --> 00:00:14,520
Aarav and his friend cycle
out of the college gate.

7
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:17,616
Both speedometers read
eighteen kilometres per hour.

8
00:00:17,966 --> 00:00:18,876
Same speed.

9
00:00:18,876 --> 00:00:22,378
But after ten minutes, they
are six kilometres apart.

10
00:00:22,378 --> 00:00:23,278
How?

11
00:00:23,382 --> 00:00:25,656
They rode in opposite directions.

12
00:00:25,656 --> 00:00:27,869
Same speed, different velocity.

13
00:00:27,869 --> 00:00:28,985
Let's see why.

14
00:00:29,635 --> 00:00:32,587
Speed tells you how
fast you cover the path.

15
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:34,719
It is distance divided by time.

16
00:00:35,069 --> 00:00:38,066
It only has a size, so speed is a scalar.

17
00:00:38,066 --> 00:00:39,865
And it is never negative.

18
00:00:40,315 --> 00:00:45,108
Velocity tells you how fast your
position changes, and in which direction.

19
00:00:45,108 --> 00:00:47,479
It is displacement divided by time.

20
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:51,537
It has a size and a direction,
so velocity is a vector.

21
00:00:51,537 --> 00:00:54,079
Both are measured in metres per second.

22
00:00:54,729 --> 00:00:56,958
Two riders, one straight road.

23
00:00:56,958 --> 00:00:58,565
The gate is at zero.

24
00:00:58,565 --> 00:00:59,892
East is positive.

25
00:01:00,242 --> 00:01:03,083
Aarav rides east at
five metres per second.

26
00:01:03,083 --> 00:01:06,230
His friend rides west at
five metres per second.

27
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:09,378
The readout shows the same speed for both.

28
00:01:09,378 --> 00:01:10,971
Five metres per second.

29
00:01:11,371 --> 00:01:14,386
But the velocities are
plus five and minus five.

30
00:01:14,386 --> 00:01:16,371
The sign carries the direction.

31
00:01:16,771 --> 00:01:18,216
Look at the graph.

32
00:01:18,216 --> 00:01:21,252
Aarav's line climbs,
his friend's line falls.

33
00:01:21,252 --> 00:01:22,469
Opposite slopes.

34
00:01:22,969 --> 00:01:25,470
Let's replay one second in slow motion.

35
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:30,362
In one second, Aarav moves five metres
east, and his friend five metres

36
00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:31,262
west.

37
00:01:31,561 --> 00:01:35,589
After a hundred and twenty seconds,
they are twelve hundred metres apart.

38
00:01:36,239 --> 00:01:39,064
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

39
00:01:39,414 --> 00:01:40,314
Step one.

40
00:01:40,309 --> 00:01:44,734
Average speed equals total path
length, divided by total time.

41
00:01:45,484 --> 00:01:46,501
Step two.

42
00:01:46,501 --> 00:01:50,618
Average velocity equals
displacement divided by time.

43
00:01:50,618 --> 00:01:53,309
v equals delta x over delta t.

44
00:01:54,059 --> 00:01:55,035
Step three.

45
00:01:55,035 --> 00:01:59,674
Distance is never less than the size
of displacement, so average speed

46
00:01:59,674 --> 00:02:03,060
is never less than the
size of average velocity.

47
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:08,139
And eighteen kilometres per hour
is exactly five metres per second.

48
00:02:08,789 --> 00:02:09,917
Remember this.

49
00:02:09,917 --> 00:02:11,382
Speed is how fast.

50
00:02:11,382 --> 00:02:14,091
Velocity is how fast, and which way.

51
00:02:14,791 --> 00:02:16,581
Why is velocity a vector?

52
00:02:16,581 --> 00:02:19,335
Let's follow the reasoning, link by link.

53
00:02:19,685 --> 00:02:20,585
Link one.

54
00:02:20,563 --> 00:02:22,426
Displacement is a vector.

55
00:02:22,426 --> 00:02:23,968
It has a direction.

56
00:02:24,368 --> 00:02:25,268
Link two.

57
00:02:25,235 --> 00:02:26,652
Time is a scalar.

58
00:02:26,652 --> 00:02:28,227
It has no direction.

59
00:02:28,627 --> 00:02:29,561
Link three.

60
00:02:29,561 --> 00:02:33,419
Dividing a vector by a positive
number keeps its direction.

61
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:36,945
So velocity points the
same way as displacement.

62
00:02:36,945 --> 00:02:39,262
That is why velocity is a vector.

63
00:02:39,862 --> 00:02:40,968
Side by side.

64
00:02:40,968 --> 00:02:45,227
Aarav rides two hundred metres
out and back, in eighty seconds.

65
00:02:45,627 --> 00:02:50,161
Average speed: four hundred metres
over eighty seconds, five metres per

66
00:02:50,161 --> 00:02:51,061
second.

67
00:02:50,831 --> 00:02:53,738
Average velocity: zero over eighty, zero.

68
00:02:54,338 --> 00:02:55,501
Now you try.

69
00:02:55,501 --> 00:02:58,864
Can a moving body have
zero average velocity?

70
00:02:58,864 --> 00:03:00,309
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:03,509 --> 00:03:04,409
Yes!

72
00:03:04,038 --> 00:03:06,445
A round trip ends where it began.

73
00:03:06,445 --> 00:03:11,073
Displacement zero, so average velocity
zero, though the speed was never

74
00:03:11,073 --> 00:03:11,973
zero.

75
00:03:12,304 --> 00:03:13,372
One more case.

76
00:03:13,372 --> 00:03:15,811
Moving in a circle at a steady speed.

77
00:03:16,161 --> 00:03:18,070
The speedometer never changes.

78
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:22,635
But the velocity arrow keeps turning,
because the direction keeps changing.

79
00:03:23,035 --> 00:03:26,553
So steady speed does not
always mean steady velocity.

80
00:03:26,553 --> 00:03:29,816
On a curve, the velocity
changes all the time.

81
00:03:30,466 --> 00:03:31,417
Your turn.

82
00:03:31,417 --> 00:03:34,511
Aarav circles a track at a constant speed.

83
00:03:34,511 --> 00:03:36,413
Is his velocity constant?

84
00:03:36,413 --> 00:03:37,812
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:41,012 --> 00:03:41,912
No!

86
00:03:41,483 --> 00:03:45,424
The size stays the same, but
the direction keeps changing.

87
00:03:45,424 --> 00:03:48,588
A change in direction
is a change in velocity.

88
00:03:49,238 --> 00:03:50,973
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:51,323 --> 00:03:54,356
Aarav rides two kilometres
east in ten minutes.

90
00:03:54,356 --> 00:03:57,851
Then he rides one kilometre
back west in five minutes.

91
00:03:58,351 --> 00:04:00,611
Total distance is three kilometres.

92
00:04:00,611 --> 00:04:04,658
Total time is fifteen minutes,
which is a quarter of an hour.

93
00:04:05,408 --> 00:04:08,354
Average speed is three
divided by a quarter.

94
00:04:08,354 --> 00:04:10,204
Twelve kilometres per hour.

95
00:04:10,954 --> 00:04:13,242
Displacement is two minus one.

96
00:04:13,242 --> 00:04:14,950
One kilometre, east.

97
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:18,894
Average velocity is one
divided by a quarter.

98
00:04:18,894 --> 00:04:21,252
Four kilometres per hour, east.

99
00:04:21,902 --> 00:04:23,368
Now, the exam trap.

100
00:04:23,718 --> 00:04:28,585
Many students think average speed is
just the size of the average velocity.

101
00:04:28,935 --> 00:04:30,186
But it is not.

102
00:04:30,186 --> 00:04:34,446
Here average speed was twelve,
and average velocity only four.

103
00:04:35,096 --> 00:04:36,509
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:36,509 --> 00:04:41,081
One step problems: round trips, parts
of a circle, journeys with stops.

105
00:04:41,681 --> 00:04:42,979
Exam level now.

106
00:04:42,979 --> 00:04:48,139
A cyclist covers half a circular track
of radius twenty one metres in eleven

107
00:04:48,139 --> 00:04:49,039
seconds.

108
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:52,936
Find the average speed and
the size of average velocity.

109
00:04:52,936 --> 00:04:54,290
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:00,941
The answer is B.

111
00:05:00,941 --> 00:05:05,580
Six metres per second, and about three
point eight two metres per second.

112
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:07,840
The distance is pi r.

113
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:11,976
Twenty two by seven times
twenty one is sixty six metres.

114
00:05:11,976 --> 00:05:15,431
Sixty six over eleven is
six metres per second.

115
00:05:15,931 --> 00:05:19,267
The displacement is the
diameter, forty two metres.

116
00:05:19,767 --> 00:05:24,235
Forty two over eleven is about three
point eight two metres per second.

117
00:05:24,835 --> 00:05:26,295
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:26,295 --> 00:05:27,797
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:30,997 --> 00:05:33,833
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:34,233 --> 00:05:35,133
One.

121
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:38,167
Why is velocity a vector,
while speed is a scalar?

122
00:05:39,067 --> 00:05:39,967
Two.

123
00:05:39,606 --> 00:05:44,065
Can a body move with a constant
speed, but a changing velocity?

124
00:05:44,965 --> 00:05:45,865
Three.

125
00:05:45,591 --> 00:05:49,440
After one full round trip,
what is the average velocity?

126
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:54,550
Next time: how do we find
velocity at one single instant?

127
00:05:54,550 --> 00:05:57,418
That is average and
instantaneous velocity.
